#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cq — the shim Codex finds on PATH.

Codex appends `<pluginRoot>/bin` to PATH for every enabled plugin, whether or not the
directory exists. This file is what makes a bare `cq` — the form every command body writes —
resolve at all. The real entry point is `scripts/bin/cq`, where it has to stay: it is cited by
absolute path from the command surface, from `hooks/hooks.json` and from the golden fixtures.

WHY THIS RE-EXECUTES INSTEAD OF IMPORTING
-----------------------------------------
`scripts/bin/cq` reaches its own `quenching` package through the `sys.path[0]` Python builds from
the *script's* directory, and that package sits beside it in `assets/bin/`. Under `runpy` — or any
other in-process form — `sys.path[0]` would be this file's directory, `bin/`, and `import
quenching` would fail. `os.execv` reproduces the exact invocation the whole surface already makes,
`python3 scripts/bin/cq ...`, so there is no behaviour to keep in sync. Replacing the process also
hands the shell cq's own exit code — the 0 ok / 1 findings / 2 refusal contract every command body
branches on — without this file touching it.

WHY THE ROOT COMES FROM `__file__`
----------------------------------
`../..` is expanded into a command body's text when the body is loaded; in a shell
it is empty, so an executable that read it to find itself would resolve to `/scripts/bin/cq`. No
executable in this plugin may depend on it to locate the plugin.
"""

import os
import sys

PLUGIN_ROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)))
REAL = os.path.join(PLUGIN_ROOT, "scripts", "bin", "cq")

os.execv(sys.executable, [sys.executable, REAL, *sys.argv[1:]])
